@article{ WOS:001297228900002,
Author = {Munawir, Ahmad and Suseno, Bambang Dwi},
Title = {Employee Performance: Exploring the Nexus of Nonstandard Services,
   Psychological Contracts, and Knowledge Sharing},
Journal = {HUMAN BEHAVIOR AND EMERGING TECHNOLOGIES},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {2024},
Month = {AUG 14},
Abstract = {Background: The interplay between the delivery of nonstandard services,
   the management of psychological contracts, and the facilitation of
   knowledge sharing forms a complex web that underpins employee
   performance in outsourcing companies. This nexus highlights the need for
   a strategic approach to human resource management that recognizes the
   unique challenges and opportunities presented by the outsourcing
   context. Leaders and managers in outsourcing companies must navigate
   these dynamics with care. Crafting clear, realistic, and flexible
   psychological contracts can provide a solid foundation for employee
   engagement. Simultaneously, fostering a culture that prizes knowledge
   sharing not only fuels innovation but also enhances the company's
   capability to deliver nonstandard services effectively. Through such
   strategies, outsourcing companies can unlock the full potential of their
   workforce, achieving not only high levels of employee performance but
   also sustainable competitive advantage in the global market.Objective:
   This research article is aimed at bridging a critical gap in the current
   body of knowledge by conducting a comprehensive investigation into the
   root causes of problems or complaints within outsourcing companies, with
   a particular focus on how these issues affect employee performance.
   Despite the recognized importance of employee performance in determining
   the success of outsourcing endeavors, there remains a significant lack
   of understanding regarding the specific factors that lead to employee
   grievances and the subsequent impact on their motivation and
   productivity. Identifying and understanding these factors is crucial for
   several reasons. First, it enables outsourcing companies to develop
   targeted strategies that address the underlying causes of employee
   dissatisfaction, rather than merely treating its symptoms. Second, by
   improving employee motivation and productivity, companies can enhance
   the quality of their services, leading to increased client satisfaction
   and competitive advantage in the market. Finally, addressing these
   issues contributes to a more positive organizational culture, which is
   essential for attracting and retaining top talent in the highly
   competitive outsourcing industry.Arrangement: This structured
   arrangement facilitates a logical flow of information, guiding readers
   through the complexities of the study while highlighting the novel
   insights it provides into improving employee performance in outsourcing
   companies through an understanding of nonstandard services,
   psychological contracts, and knowledge sharing.Method: This study adopts
   a quantitative research methodology, framed within a correlational
   design, to scrutinize the interrelations among various factors affecting
   employee performance in the manufacturing sector. It specifically
   investigates how psychological contracts (X1) and knowledge sharing
   (X2), as exogenous variables, relate to employee performance (Z), an
   endogenous variable. The analysis also considers the mediating role of
   nonstandard services (Y), examining both direct and indirect effects on
   employee performance. The objective is to uncover the complex dynamics
   between these variables and their collective impact on employee
   outcomes. For participant selection, the study utilizes the proportional
   random sampling technique, which is particularly effective in
   populations with relatively uniform characteristics, such as those found
   within the manufacturing industry a number 299 respondent.
   This sampling method ensures that the sample accurately reflects the
   broader population, contributing to the generalizability of the study's
   findings. From the four variables in this study measured by 30
   indicators, the minimum sample size required is determined at 30x10=300,
   so the sample size needed is around 300 samples. In this study, the
   sample size obtained was 299 after an assessment, all eligible to be
   processed through data testing. This study uses proportional random
   sampling for participant selection because it is suitable for a
   relatively homogeneous population.Results: The research findings
   highlight the critical roles of psychological contracts and knowledge
   sharing in bolstering organizational effectiveness, employee engagement,
   and performance. Psychological contracts significantly influence
   employee behavior, engagement, and knowledge sharing, which are
   essential for enhancing nonstandard services, service delivery, and
   fostering a conducive work environment. This environment, in turn,
   stimulates creativity, innovation, and improved service quality. The
   strategic management of psychological contracts, aligned with
   organizational goals, and the implementation of effective knowledge
   management practices, are vital for promoting a culture of knowledge
   sharing. Together, these strategies support employee well-being,
   facilitate performance improvement, and drive organizational success by
   prioritizing nonstandard service enhancements. These insights underscore
   the necessity of integrating strong psychological contracts and
   knowledge sharing practices into business governance to achieve desired
   outcomes and organizational effectiveness.Conclusion: Psychological
   contracts significantly influence employee behavior and organizational
   outcomes, including both nonstandard services and employee performance.
   By shaping how employees engage with their work and the organization at
   large, psychological contracts are pivotal in driving engagement,
   knowledge sharing behaviors, and, ultimately, organizational
   effectiveness and performance enhancement. This underscores the critical
   nature of fostering robust psychological contracts and ensuring they are
   well-aligned with organizational objectives to boost employee
   performance and achieve the desired outcomes. Furthermore, knowledge
   sharing emerges as a critical factor within organizations, playing a
   dual role. It not only enhances employee performance by contributing to
   improved productivity and organizational effectiveness but also plays a
   pivotal role in enhancing the delivery of nonstandard services. Through
   the dissemination of knowledge and expertise, organizations can innovate
   and improve their service offerings, leading to better organizational
   effectiveness. Moreover, the provision of nonstandard services within
   organizations is highlighted as a significant contributor to enhancing
   employee performance. This leads to improved productivity and, by
   extension, organizational effectiveness. The relationship between
   nonstandard services and employee performance illustrates the importance
   of innovative and tailored service solutions in driving organizational
   success.Contribution: Overall, these findings highlight the intertwined
   roles of psychological contracts, knowledge sharing, and nonstandard
   services in shaping employee behavior and improving organizational
   outcomes. By prioritizing these elements, organizations can foster a
   culture of engagement, innovation, and high performance, leading to
   sustained organizational effectiveness and success.
   The research findings underscore the critical importance of strategic
   management of psychological contracts in shaping employee behaviors and
   organizational outcomes. Establishing positive psychological contracts
   can enhance employee engagement and commitment, ultimately leading to
   organizational success. Future research endeavors should consider
   exploring the longitudinal effects of psychological contracts, knowledge
   sharing practices, and nonstandard services on organizational outcomes.
   Longitudinal studies offer the opportunity to observe how these factors
   evolve over time and their enduring impact on organizational
   effectiveness.},
